Sucrose is transported from sources to sinks in specialised vascular tissue. Fig. 2 shows the vascular tissue used for the transport of sucrose in plants.

Tissue **B** in Fig. 2 contains many mitochondria.

Explain how this assists in the loading of sucrose into the sieve tube.

Mitochondria aid the loading of sucrose into the sieve tube as follows:

- (Mitochondria are) the site of ATP production (during aerobic respiration);
- ATP powers H^+^/proton pumps in the walls of the sieve tube;
- H^+^ ions are pumped from the companion cell/**B** into the cell walls (creating a H^+^ ion diffusion gradient);
- The H^+^ ions diffuse back into the companion cell via cotransporter proteins;
- Cotransporter proteins transport sucrose molecules into the companion cell against the (sucrose) concentration gradient;
- Sucrose moves into the sieve tube by diffusion / down the (sucrose) concentration gradient;

Explain how the process in part (c) enables mass flow of sucrose in the sieve tube.

